<h2>The facts, briefly</h2>
<p>In October 2025, a hospital doctor reported that a minor had delivered a baby, leading to FIR No. 72/2025 at Police Station Shalteng, Srinagar. The accused, a distant relative of the girl, was arrested on 7 October 2025. DNA testing confirmed he was the biological father of the newborn; the victim was sixteen years, three months and eleven days old at delivery. A chargesheet under Section 64 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 and Sections 3 and 4 of the POCSO Act, 2012 was filed on 3 December 2025, and charges were framed on 28 September 2026.</p>
<p>The victim then moved the High Court under Section 528 BNSS — the successor to Section 482 CrPC — seeking quashing of the chargesheet. She asserted that the relationship was consensual, that a nikah had been performed, and that she wished to live with the accused as his wife. The State opposed: a minor had no locus to invoke the extraordinary jurisdiction without a guardian, disputed facts could not be resolved in a quashing petition, and the consent of a child is no consent in law.</p>

<h2>Three holdings</h2>
<div class="tiles">
<div class="tile"><strong>Consent below eighteen is immaterial.</strong> The victim was below the statutory age of consent, so the consensual character of the relationship could not take the case out of POCSO. The court also noted that the accused was aware of her age, foreclosing any argument of innocent mistake.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>Mitigation is for bail, not quashing.</strong> The nikah, the pregnancy, the position of the families and the victim's move to her matrimonial home were, in the court's words, mitigating factors entitling the accused to seek bail — but they cannot be reason to quash the chargesheet. Prosecution and punishment travel on separate tracks from sympathy.</div>
<div class="tile"><strong>A minor cannot sue alone in inherent jurisdiction.</strong> The petitioner, herself still a minor, had filed without a guardian or next friend. The court held she was not competent to invoke its extraordinary jurisdiction, an independent ground for dismissal.</div>
</div>

<h2>The reasoning behind the line</h2>
<p>POCSO is built on a legislative judgment that persons under eighteen cannot give legally operative consent to sexual activity. Once the age is established, the offence under Sections 3 and 4 is complete irrespective of the dynamics between the parties; here, the DNA report and the medical record left little room for factual dispute. Courts exercising inherent powers can quash proceedings that are an abuse of process, but a prosecution squarely supported by scientific evidence, filed and charged, is the opposite of an abuse — it is the statute operating as designed.</p>
<p>The judgment is also candid about the human cost. It noticed the growing judicial commentary, including from the Supreme Court, on the criminalisation of adolescent relationships and the calls for a legislative Romeo-Juliet exception. But it drew the constitutional boundary precisely: the plea for reform is addressed to Parliament, and until the statute changes, a High Court cannot achieve by quashing what the legislature has declined to enact.</p>

<div class="note">
<p>The locus holding deserves separate attention from practitioners. Petitions on behalf of minors — whether victims or accused — must be instituted through a guardian or next friend. A petition filed by a minor alone risks dismissal without ever reaching the merits, whatever its substantive strength.</p>
</div>

<h2>Where sympathy still operates</h2>
<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>Bail.</strong> The same circumstances that cannot quash a chargesheet — marriage, a child, family acceptance, the victim's own stance — are legitimate considerations in a bail application, where courts weigh flight risk, tampering and the realities of continued detention.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Trial.</strong> The victim's testimony remains to be recorded, and the trial court will decide the case on the evidence led before it, not on the chargesheet alone.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Sentence.</strong> Where conviction follows, mitigating circumstances inform the sentencing discretion to the extent the statute permits.</div>
</div>

<h2>The takeaway for Delhi practice</h2>
<p>Delhi courts see the same pattern with regularity: an adolescent relationship, an FIR set in motion by a mandatory report, and later attempts — often by the victim or her family — to fold the prosecution into a marriage. The consistent judicial answer, in Delhi as in Srinagar, is that POCSO offences are not compoundable and cannot be settled or married away, while bail and sentencing remain the lawful outlets for genuinely mitigating circumstances. Families navigating such cases need advice that distinguishes clearly between what a court may do and what it must refuse, before irreversible procedural choices are made.</p>
